  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>One very common reaction of amide is Hoffmann&#39;s bromamide reaction in which amide get converted into amine having one carbon less than amide. For this reaction reagent is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>Br</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>KOH</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>or</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>KOBr</mi><mo>)</mo></math>.</p><p>Lithium aluminium hydride is very strong reducing agent which will convert amide into amine. Number of carbon is same in both amide and amine.</p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0104_066e11f606d2.png\" style=\"width: 445px; height: 322px;\" /></p>"
  }
